content:
I've reviewed hundreds of codebases over the past few years, and the same security blind spots appear everywhere. Here are the ones that trip up even experienced developers.

## 1. Client-Side Trust

The most dangerous assumption in web development: **never trust the client**. Every validation you do on the frontend is a UX feature, not a security feature.

```javascript
// This is NOT security
if (userInput.length > 100) {
  alert('Input too long');
  return;
}

// This IS security
if (req.body.input.length > 100) {
  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Input too long' });
}
```

## 2. Secret Management

Developers leak secrets in the most unexpected places:
- Git history (even after deletion)
- Error logs
- Client-side bundles
- Environment variable dumps in debug mode
- Docker images

Use a proper secrets manager. Rotate keys regularly. Audit your git history.

## 3. Authentication ≠ Authorization

Just because a user is authenticated doesn't mean they're authorized to access a resource. I see this bug in nearly every codebase:

```typescript
// WRONG: Only checks authentication
app.get('/api/user/:id', authenticate, (req, res) => {
  const user = db.getUser(req.params.id);
  res.json(user);
});

// RIGHT: Checks both auth AND authorization
app.get('/api/user/:id', authenticate, authorize('read:user'), (req, res) => {
  const user = db.getUser(req.params.id);
  res.json(user);
});
```

## 4. Dependency Vulnerabilities

Your dependencies are your attack surface. Run `npm audit` regularly. Set up automated alerts. Don't ignore warnings because "it's just a dev dependency" — dev dependencies get bundled in production builds more often than you think.

## 5. Input Sanitization

SQL injection is so 2010, right? Wrong. It's still one of the most common vulnerabilities. And NoSQL injection is its equally dangerous cousin.

**Use parameterized queries. Always. No exceptions.**

The best security mindset is paranoia combined with systematic thinking. Assume everything is a vulnerability until proven otherwise.
